  "issues": {
    "auth_failed": {
      "title": "ZTE router sign-in failed",
      "fix_flow": {
        "step": {
          "confirm": {
            "title": "Re-enter the router credentials",
            "description": "{name} could not sign in to the router: the stored username or password was refused.\n\nContinue to re-enter the credentials.\n\nIf they are unchanged and still refused, check whether the router's password was changed on the device itself, and whether the account is locked out after repeated failed sign-ins."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "conn_error": {
      "title": "ZTE router is not responding",
      "description": "{name} has failed to reach the router at {host} on {count} consecutive attempts, so its entities are unavailable.\n\nTen failures in a row means the problem is not clearing on its own. Common causes, roughly in order of likelihood:\n\n- **The router is powered off, or has locked up.** Power it off, wait a few seconds, and power it back on.\n- **Its IP address has changed.** Many routers get a new address after a power cut or a DHCP lease change. Check the router's current address and, if it differs from `{host}`, use **Reconfigure** on the integration to update it. A static DHCP reservation prevents this recurring.\n- **The login details have changed.** If the router's password was changed, use **Reconfigure** to enter the new one.\n- **The network path is broken** — a cable, switch, VLAN or firewall rule between Home Assistant and the router.\n\nThis message clears automatically as soon as the router answers again."
    }
  },
